# Lesson 6, Mike's Exercise 3, turned so the student (South) is on lead. East deals here, so dummy's jump raise is an unpassed hand.
title: Your Exercise 3: a singleton
id: L06-06
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.8
student: S
board: 6

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  6      T98    T83     AT9542
  E  KQ984  AK7    K4      Q83
  S  T72    J532   A9762   6
  W  AJ53   Q64    QJ5     KJ7

auction:
  E 1S     = 13+ points, 4+ spades
  S Pass   = Not enough to bid
  W 3S     = An opening hand or better with 4+ spades: game is on
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 4S     = Game in spades
  S Pass   = Nothing to add
  W Pass   = Done
  N Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1♠ and West's jump to 3♠ showed an opening hand with four or
  more spades, so East bid the game. East is declarer, and so you, South,
  will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 13-21 = East opened one of a suit: 13 to 21.
  dummy: 13-27 = West's jump raise showed an opening hand or better.
  partner: 0-9 = 40, minus your 5 and the opponents' 26 or more, leaves at most 9.
  remind: You hold three trumps. Could a short suit turn one of them into a trick?

play:
  1   S C6*  W C7  N CA  E C3
      C6 key: A singleton in a side suit. If partner can win a club, you ruff the next one: a cheap trick from your small trumps.
      C6 not D: Leading away from an ace against a suit contract can give declarer a trick with the king. Your ♦A is a sure trick: save it.
      C6 not DA: Cashing the ♦A is safe, but it builds nothing. Your singleton club could earn a ruff.
      C6 not H: From J532 a heart is a guess. Your singleton club has a plan.
      C6 not S: Your small trumps are worth more ruffing clubs than leading out.
      note: Partner wins the ♣A. With six clubs in her hand and three in dummy, she can tell your ♣6 is probably a singleton.
  2   N C4  E C8  S S2  W CJ
      note: Partner returns a club and you ruff: a trick from one of your small trumps.
  3   S DA  W D5  N D3  E D4
      note: Now cash your ♦A.
  4   S D7  W DJ  N D8  E DK
      note: Nothing else to cash, so you get out with a diamond.
  5   E SK  S S7  W S3  N S6
      note: Declarer draws trumps.
  6   E SQ  S ST  W S5  N C2
      note: Your last trump goes.
  7   E H7  S H2  W HQ  N H8
      note: Declarer runs the hearts.
  8   W H4  N H9  E HA  S H3
  9   E HK  S H5  W H6  N HT
  10  E CQ  S D2  W CK  N C5
      note: Declarer's clubs win one trick between them.
  11  W SA  N C9  E S4  S D6
      note: The rest are declarer's.
  12  W SJ  N CT  E S8  S D9
  13  W DQ  N DT  E S9  S HJ

play recap:
  Your singleton club worked as hoped: partner won the ♣A, and your ruff
  plus the ♦A gave the defense three tricks. Declarer still made 4♠, but
  without the ruff he would have made an overtrick. To beat it, partner
  would have needed one more way in for a second ruff. A singleton lead
  needs two things: a partner who can win the trick, and trumps of your own
  to ruff with before declarer draws them.
